  "robustness_conclusion": "Under continuous true buying intent, fixed decision thresholds exhibit severe boundary sensitivity: 1. QualLoop's false-pause rate rises dramatically from 1.4% (2/140) on discrete clusters to 23.5% (8/34) on continuous intent, as small-sample sampling variance near the hurdle causes profitable campaigns to be paused prematurely.\n2. Policy thresholds do not hold near the hurdle: in the marginal good stratum [0.15, 0.22], QualLoop pauses 24.2% of good ads, while in the marginal bad stratum [0.11, 0.15], it misses 48.0% of unprofitable ads (pausing only 52.0%).\n3. QualLoop's realized cost per qualified lead (Rs 571.68; QL/Rs = 0.001749) is strictly worse than naive Baseline C's (Rs 535.50; QL/Rs = 0.001867), because QualLoop's conservative credible interval delays pausing marginal poor ads while still penalizing marginal good ads.\n4. The hurdle sensitivity sweep (hurdles 0.10, 0.15, 0.20) confirms that altering the hurdle shifts the trade-off along the operating curve without resolving boundary instability."
